# Finalise Commands

- Map `finalise` to `npm run finalise`.
- Map `finalise full` to `npm run finalise:full`.
- Map `finalise and push` and `fap` / `/fap` to COMPLETE_AND_RELEASE(normal): ordinary work runs `npm run finalise`, then a normal fetch/ancestry check and fast-forward push. Protected CRITICAL / C9 work uses `npm run finalise:push`.
- Map `finalise full and push` and `ffap` / `/ffap` to COMPLETE_AND_RELEASE(full): ordinary work runs `npm run finalise:full`, then a normal fetch/ancestry check and fast-forward push. Protected CRITICAL / C9 work uses `npm run finalise:full:push`.
- Use those authorized push phrases (`finalise and push`, `fap` / `/fap`, `finalise full and push`, `ffap` / `/ffap`, `finalise:push`, or `push to GitHub` as applicable) only when the user wrote them as the current action. They implement the global COMPLETE_AND_RELEASE contract and must not be redefined as local-only. Do not invoke `finalise:push` / `finalise:full:push` for ordinary work; those scripts keep refusing ordinary `--push` before mutation.
- Before any push, report the branch, commits, and changed files that will be pushed.
- Check for an existing finalise process before starting another.
- Never push after a failed build, test, cleanup, commit, or release-metadata step.
- Treat a failed finalise as a diagnostic run, not the start of a full-suite retry loop:
  1. Read the complete failure summary and collect every reported failure.
  2. Repair all known related failures in one consolidated sweep.
  3. Re-run only the failed test files, test names, projects, or failed build/check until they pass reliably.
  4. Freeze the candidate, then run the original `finalise` or `finalise:full` command once as the closure gate.
- If that closure run reveals new failures, return to targeted diagnosis and verification. Do not immediately launch another full finalise.
- Use `finalise:repair` only when its selected step is itself the narrowest meaningful failed check. Do not use it when it would rerun a broad suite that can be targeted directly.
- A broad rerun before targeted stability is allowed only when the failure cannot be isolated or is demonstrably order-/suite-dependent; record that reason first.
- Never rerun an unchanged full suite after failure, and never run full finalise after fixing only one failure while other reported failures remain unresolved.
- Read the generated `docs_private/automation/runs/finalise/*.md` summary and report its local path.
- Treat a post-product-commit release failure as fail-closed: preserve local work, do not push, and follow the printed recovery command.
- Local commit is not release-ready, and release-ready is not a push.
- Run `npm run workflow-protocol -- status --blocking` before mutating finalise. Valid split ancestors are parked history. The active descendant owns remaining work. After two failed premium rounds, remaining work is routing, isolation, or proven removal from release — not another normal final-diff pass. Do not hand-edit protocol JSON.
- Do not launch a third premium review for the same CRITICAL continuation. Routing or split does not reset this budget.
- Once unresolved CRITICAL implementation is committed to the current release branch, do not start unrelated implementation on that branch. Finish CRITICAL first or use a separate explicitly-authorised branch/worktree.
